## What this covers

- `diagnostic_benchmark/` — the diagnostic corpus, written by the authors:
  58 requirements, 110 controls, 86 links, and the `match_type` vocabulary
  regime labels.
- The derived CSVs produced by the corpus builders in `csf_benchmark/`,
  `hipaa_benchmark/` and `pf_benchmark/`: our selection, parsing, control
  rendering, parameter resolution and link normalisation of the NIST sources.
- The `provenance.json` files and the CPRT snapshots under
  `hipaa_benchmark/cprt_snapshots/`.

## What this does not cover

The underlying NIST artifacts are United States Government works and are in
the public domain, not ours to license:

- SP 800-53 Revision 5 control catalogue (OSCAL)
- CSF 1.1 to SP 800-53r5 crosswalk
- SP 800-66 Revision 2 and the HIPAA Security Rule OLIR crosswalk
- NIST Privacy Framework to SP 800-53r5 crosswalk

Our contribution to those three corpora is the extraction and encoding, not
the text or the mappings. `SOURCES.md` lists every source URL with its
SHA-256 and records what each builder dropped, skipped or resolved.

The diagnostic corpus contains requirement statements paraphrased from
publicly available frameworks (ISO 27001, PCI DSS, SOC 2) as author-written
summaries. They are not reproductions of the standards' text, and the
standards themselves are not redistributed here.

